<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Plateworks Ignition collects no data and does no tracking. It works
         fully offline; UserDefaults is used only to remember the user's own
         last-used inputs on-device.

         Precise location: the app can take a single, user-initiated GPS fix to
         fill the observation site's coordinate and elevation (when-in-use only;
         it never tracks). That location is NOT listed under
         NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes because it is never transmitted off the
         device — it is written to the on-device observation log and appears only
         in spreadsheets the user themselves chooses to export or share. Apple
         defines "collected" as transmitted off-device for developer access,
         which this is not. If the app ever gains a network feature that sends a
         position anywhere, this array must gain
         NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePreciseLocation.

         Device backups: to be precise rather than merely reassuring — the
         observation log, including any GPS coordinates on it, IS included in
         iCloud and encrypted local backups, like any app's stored data. That is
         ordinary iOS behaviour and is not collection by the developer, but it is
         a weaker claim than "the data never leaves the phone", so say the
         accurate one. The log is deliberately NOT marked
         isExcludedFromBackup: losing a shift record to a phone replacement is a
         worse outcome for a crew than the record existing in their own backup. -->
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>
    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>
    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array/>
    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <!-- CA92.1: access info stored by this app, on this device. -->
                <string>CA92.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
